f opponent is weak, but since they re-raised pre-flop, they likely have something decent; they might call a small bet and beat us. Our Ace-high could actually be best if opponent missed completely (like QJ or small pocket pairs that didn’t improve), but betting could make them fold those hands and only continue with better, which is bad.  \n\nTherefore, checking keeps the pot small, lets us see a free river, then we can reassess.  \n\n---\n\n**Reason:**  \nWe are out of position if opponent bets river after our turn check, but given the history of opponent checking twice after flop, they probably won’t bet large on river unless they improve. Our hand has showdown value as Ace-high if opponent missed, so we can call a small river bet. Betting now risks being raised or called by better hands without fold equity, since board is dry and not scary for opponent.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer:** 0'}
